sex, some violence, and some strong language. Bestseller

1999.

RC 49307.

##



The Lion's Game.

RC 49310.

by Nelson DeMille.

read by Fred Major.

5 cassettes.

John Corey, last seen in _Plum Island (RC 44551)_, and his

FBI mentor Kate Mayfield are at the New York airport to

meet a Libyan terrorist nicknamed "the lion." But the

aircraft lands with all of the passengers dead and the lion

missing. Violence, strong language, and some explicit

descriptions of sex. Bestseller 2000.

RC 49310.

##



High Tide.

RC 49414.

by Jude Deveraux.

read by Madelyn Buzzard.

2 cassettes.

Fashion doll creator Fiona Burkenhalter aims to secure an

account with children's author Roy Hudson during a Florida

fishing trip that is guided by Ace Montgomery. When the

excursion goes awry, Fiona and Ace stand accused of

murdering Hudson. Some descriptions of sex and some strong

language. Bestseller 1999. 1999.

RC 49414.

##



The Land beyond the Mountains.

RC 49418.

by Janice Holt Giles.

read by Mitzi Friedlander.

3 cassettes.

Kentucky Territory, 1783. Young Major Cass Cartwright

leaves his Virginia home to establish a settlement in the

wilderness. His partner is General James Wilkinson, a

roguish schemer who conspires with the Spanish to establish

his own empire. Cartwright, however, remains loyal to his

country while being torn between two women. 1958.

RC 49418.

##



Presidential Deal.

RC 49419.

by Les Standiford.

read by Roy Avers.

2 cassettes.

Florida contractor John Deal is about to receive a medal

from the first lady for rescuing drowning Cuban refugees,

when terrorists invade the ceremony. The two are taken to a

Caribbean island stronghold, where a hurricane strikes. As

they struggle to escape, Deal's friends continue to

investigate. Strong language and violence. 1998.

RC 49419.

##



Murder in Georgetown: An Eleanor Roosevelt Mystery.

RC 49435.

by Elliott Roosevelt.

read by Janis Gray.

2 cassettes.

Washington, D.C.; 1935. When Sargent Peavy, a member of the

Federal Reserve Board, is murdered, police and his widow

accuse his lover, Jessica Dee. But Jessica has been working

as a spy for First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt on the staff of

Senator Huey Long. Eleanor becomes involved in clearing

Jessica's name. 1999. 

RC 49435.

##



The Sea of Grass.

RC 49473.

by Conrad Richter.

read by Bob Askey.

1 cassette.

Lutie Brewton leaves her husband and children on the Cross

B ranch to return to the city. Twenty years later she finds

a reason to come back when her youngest son, Brock, is shot

as a desperado. 1936.

RC 49473.

##



The Looking Glass.

RC 49474.

by Richard Paul Evans.

read by Jim Zeiger.

1 cassette.

Devastated over his wife's death in childbirth, embittered

minister Hunter Bell sets out for the Utah gold country and

strikes it rich. But it is Bell's discovery of an abused

woman in the snow outside his cottage that sets them both

on the path of healing. Some violence. Bestseller 1999.

RC 49474.

##



Hunting Badger.

RC 49475.

by Tony Hillerman.

read by Bob Askey.

2 cassettes.

A casino robbery on the Ute reservation leaves one guard

dead, while a second is wounded and held under suspicion.

As Navajo Tribal Police sergeant Jim Chee tracks the

criminals in the Four Corners canyons, retired cop Joe

Leaphorn receives a startling tip identifying the

perpetrators. Some violence. Bestseller 1999.

RC 49475.

##



Tom Clancy's Net Force: Hidden Agendas.

RC 49476.

by Tom Clancy and Steve Pieczenik.

read by Chuck Benson.

2 cassettes.

2010. When top secret intelligence appears on the Internet,

FBI Net Force chief Alex Michaels is summoned before a

hostile Senate committee. Then Michaels and his Net Force

sleuths pursue a trail of evidence that leads back home.

Sequel to _Tom Clancy's Net Force (RC 47916)_. Strong

language and some violence. Bestseller  1999.

RC 49476.

##



Dust Devils.

RC 49479.

by Robert Laxalt.

read by Bob Askey.

1 cassette.

Ira Hamilton wins an Arabian colt at the rodeo with the

help of his Paiute friend, Cricket. When rustlers steal the

horse, Ira tracks them across the desert, almost dying from

thirst, flash floods, and a bullet. Some strong language,

some violence, and some descriptions of sex. For senior

high and older readers. 1997.

RC 49479.

##



The Best Intentions.

RC 49480.

by Candice Hern.

read by Kristin Allison.

2 cassettes.

Miles Prescott, the Earl of Strickland, needs a mother for

his two children. When his sister arrives for a visit, she

brings Lady Abingdon, a young widow, for his perusal. But

it is the lady's uncouth but scholarly sister Hannah whom

Miles and his family embrace. 1999. 1999.

RC 49480.

##



Jewels of the Sun: The Gallaghers of Ardmore, Book 1.

RC 49482.

by Nora Roberts.

read by Martha Harmon Pardee.

2 cassettes.

A depressed Jude Murray flees Chicago for her ancestral

cottage in Ardmore, Ireland. She soon discovers Gallagher's

Pub and the siblings who run it--Darcy and her brothers,

Shawn and Aidan. Jude encounters banshees and faeries, and

also falls in love. Some explicit descriptions of sex and

some strong language. Bestseller 1999.

RC 49482.

##



The Cat Who Robbed a Bank.

RC 49500.

by Lilian Jackson Braun.

read by Bob Askey.

2 cassettes.

Autumn in Pickax brings festivals, the Scottish Games, and

the return of estate jeweler Mr. Delacamp. But Mr. Delacamp

is murdered in the newly restored hotel, and his assistant

and the desk clerk are missing. Qwilleran takes clues from

his cats, Koko and Yum Yum, to solve the crime. Bestseller

1999. 1999.

RC 49500.

##



The Bluest Eye.

RC 49914.

by Toni Morrison.

read by Pam Ward.

2 cassettes (Reissue).

1941. Eleven-year-old Pecola Breedlove--poor, ugly, and

black--desperately wants blue eyes, which she thinks would

solve all her problems. But instead she is subjected to

rejection, violence, and an unwanted pregnancy. Slowly, she

begins to descend into madness. Strong language and some

explicit descriptions of sex. Bestseller 1970.

RC 49914.

##



###

_Books for Children_

Books listed in this issue of _Talking Book Topics_ were

recently sent to cooperating libraries.  The complete

collection contains books by many authors on fiction and

nonfiction subjects, including animals, geography, nature,

mystery, sports, and others.  Contact your cooperating

library to learn more about the wide range of books

available in the collection.  Cassette books are labeled

with the code RC and play at 15/16 ips.  To order books,

contact your cooperating library.



_Cassettes Nonfiction_



Lives of the Artists: Masterpieces, Messes (and What the

Neighbors Thought).

RC 48267.

by Kathleen Krull.

read by Conrad Feininger.

1 cassette.

Portraits of twenty artists, providing information about

their childhood years and the works that made them famous.

Includes Michelangelo Buonarroti, Rembrandt van Rijn, Mary

Cassatt, Henri Matisse, Georgia O'Keeffe, and Andy Warhol.

For grades 3-6. 1995.

RC 48267.

##



My Life in Dog Years.

RC 48269.

by Gary Paulsen.

read by Michael Kramer.

1 cassette.

Paulsen proudly refers to himself as a "dog person,"

someone who loves dogs, and always has at least five or

six. He writes about eight of the especially memorable dogs

that have shared his life through the years. In the

dedication to Cookie, he tells how she saved his life in

1980 when he had fallen through ice. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 48269.

##



Terror of the Spanish Main: Sir Henry Morgan and His

Buccaneers.

RC 48281.

by Albert Marrin.

read by Bill Wallace.

2 cassettes.

Depicts the seventeenth-century world and the life of Henry

Morgan. Portrays a self-made man who rose from a humble

home in Wales to be knighted by the Queen of England and to

become lieutenant governor of Jamaica. Describes his

plundering of Spanish towns along the Central and South

American mainland. For grades 6-9. 1999.

RC 48281.

##



Fifty-Two Days by Camel: My Sahara Adventure.

RC 48295.

by Lawrie Raskin.

read by Erik Sandvold.

1 cassette.

Describes the author's journey through Morocco, across the

Atlas Mountains, into the Sahara Desert, arriving at

Timbuktu, and continuing to ancient salt mines in a remote

part of the desert. Discusses camels, nomads, customs,

Muslim traditions, and his adventures traveling in his

favorite terrain--the desert. For grades 4-7. 1998.

RC 48295.

##



Tracking Dinosaurs in the Gobi.

RC 48447.

by Margery Facklam.

read by Gary Telles.

1 cassette.

A history of expeditions searching for dinosaur fossils in

the Gobi Desert of Mongolia. Describes the discovery of

dinosaur eggs in 1923 by Roy Chapman Andrews's team, which

caused enduring interest in the area. Explains the complex

logistics of mounting a scientific field trip in the 1920s

and in the 1990s. For grades 5-8. 1997.

RC 48447.

##



The Composite Guide to Auto Racing.

RC 48508.

by John F. Wukovits.

read by Marilyn Gleason.

1 cassette.

The history of auto racing from its inception to its 1990s

popularity in the United States. Includes a chronology. For

grades 4-7. 1999.

RC 48508.

##



War and the Pity of War.

RC 48626.

edited by Neil Philip.

read by Jeff Baker.

1 cassette.

International anthology of seventy-one poems written across

the ages depicting conflicts from eleventh century B.C.

China to Serbo-Croatia in 1993. Selections reveal both the

noble aspirations and the physical horrors of war, and

soldiering as a common experience throughout human history.

For grades 6-9 and older readers. 1998.

RC 48626.

##



Mariah Carey.

RC 48662.

by Melanie Cole.

read by Celeste Lawson.

1 cassette.

Describes singer Mariah Carey's life and how she overcame

prejudice to become a popular entertainer. Mariah's mother,

an opera singer, encouraged her daughter's musical talent

and realized that, even as a toddler, Mariah had perfect

pitch. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 48662.

##



When Dinosaurs Die: A Guide to Understanding Death.

RC 48663.

by Laurie Krasny Brown and Marc Brown.

read by MaryBeth Wise.

1 cassette.

Presents a look at how children are affected by death,

especially the loss of a loved one. Explains what it means

to be alive and what death is. Discusses the different

reasons people die, how to say good-bye, and ways to

remember someone. For grades K-3. 1996.

RC 48663.

##



 Buffalo Soldiers.

RC 48667.

by Catherine Reef.

read by Chuck Young.

1 cassette.

Recalls the history of African American soldiers who made

up the 9th and 10th Cavalry. After the Civil War (1861-

1865), black soldiers were sent as a peacekeeping force to

the western frontier. Describes the regiments and their

campaigns in Mexico and Cuba, and the 92nd Infantry's work

in Europe. For grades 4-7. 1993.

RC 48667.

##



Earth, Fire, Water, Air.

RC 48673.

by Mary Hoffman.

read by Susan McInerney.

1 cassette.

Recounts myths, legends, poems, proverbs, and superstitions

about each of the four elements of the title, as expressed

in various cultural traditions. Intended to encourage

respect for the natural world. For grades 4-7. 1995.

RC 48673.

##



Roberto Alomar: An Authorized Biography.

RC 49003.

by Norman L. Macht.

read by Gary Telles.

1 cassette.

Highlights the career of the Puerto Rican baseball player,

emphasizing the hard work and dedication to the sport that

lead to his success. Depicts his childhood in a family of

athletes, his disappointments and struggles in the minor

league, his World Series play, and his suspension. For

grades 4-7. 1999.

RC 49003.

##



Sammy Sosa.

RC 49004.

by Carrie Muskat.

read by Gary Telles.

1 cassette.

Biography of the record-breaking baseball player from the

Dominican Republic. Sammy Sosa was raised in poverty, but

his athletic ability led him to his first professional

sports contract at age sixteen. Sosa also became well known

for his generosity in providing assistance to his family

and homeland. For grades 5-8. 1999.

RC 49004.

##



Cliques, Phonies, and Other Baloney.

RC 49137.

by Trevor Romain.

read by Kerry Cundiff.

1 cassette.

Humorously discusses the negative aspects of exclusive in-

groups. Explains how to make genuine friends and what to do

in different social situations. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49137.

##



Bayard Rustin: Behind the Scenes of the Civil Rights

Movement.

RC 49213.

by James Haskins.

read by Bill Wallace.

1 cassette.

Biography of the skillful political organizer of the huge

March on Washington in 1963. Explains how he acquired his

beliefs in nonviolent protest and how he later influenced

Martin Luther King Jr., starting at the 1955 bus boycott in

Montgomery, Alabama. For grades 5-8. 1997.

RC 49213.

##



Let's Talk about When a Parent Dies.

RC 49392.

by Elizabeth Weitzman.

read by Rick Foucheux.

1 cassette.

Discusses various feelings and reactions a child may

experience after the death of a parent. Offers advice on

how to deal with these emotions and how to adjust to this

new situation. For grades K-3. 1996.

RC 49392.

##



Let's Talk about Going to a Funeral.

RC 49431.

by Marianne Johnston.

read by Jill Fox.

1 cassette.

Explains what a funeral is and what happens at the ceremony

and the cemetery. Discusses people's feelings of loss and

how they express grief. For grades K-3. 1997.

RC 49431.

##



Canada.

RC 49443.

by Bob Barlas and Norman Tompsett.

read by Patricia McDermott.

1 cassette.

Describes the nation of Canada, which shares its border

with the United States. Includes geography and history,

government and people, arts and leisure, as well as its

relations with the United States. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49443.

##



Under the Royal Palms: A Childhood in Cuba.

RC 49485.

by Alma Flor Ada.

read by Gabriella Cavallero.

1 cassette.

In this companion to _Where the Flame Trees Bloom (RC

42823)_ the author continues the story of her childhood in

a small town in Cuba. She reminisces about her uncles, a

pilot and a doctor, and about the death of her beloved

ballet teacher. For grades 3-6. 1998.

RC 49485.

##



###

_Cassettes Fiction_



The Deetkatoo: Native American Stories about Little People.

RC 47326.

compiled by John Bierhorst.

read by Erik Sandvold.

1 cassette.

Twenty-two stories about the little people whose strength

and wisdom transcend their size. A Cherokee tale, "The

Little House in the Deep Water," explains why the little

people can be heard talking below the water's surface. The

title piece, told by the Tillamook, shows how a little man

helps a woman regain her husband. For grades 4-7 and older

readers. 1998.

RC 47326.

##



A Glory of Unicorns.

RC 47880.

compiled by Bruce Coville.

read by Lindsay Ellison.

1 cassette.

Short stories and a poem celebrating unicorns. In _The Ugly

Unicorn_, a blind girl in ancient China believes a homely

creature is a gorgeous unicorn. In _The Healing Truth_, no

one believes chronic liar Crystal when she reports that a

unicorn lives in the supply closet. For grades 4-7. 1998.

RC 47880.

##



The Long Ride.

RC 48023.

by Bonnie Bryant.

read by Jill Fox.

1 cassette.

Horse-crazy girlfriends Stevie, Lisa, and Carole spend all

their free time at Pine Hollow Stables. Callie, a new girl,

causes trouble, and an accident leads to tragedy. The girls

all learn to band together and participate in therapeutic

riding. For grades 6-9. 1998.

RC 48023.

##



Her Blue Straw Hat.

RC 48024.

by C.S. Adler.

read by Anne Mullen.

1 cassette.

Rachel is looking forward to spending the summer on Cape

Cod at her stepfather's cottage. She gets along fine with

Ben--even better than she does with her mother, "M.J." But

M.J. and Ben have some surprises in store for Rachel, and

her summer may not be so great after all. For grades 5-8.

1997.

RC 48024.

##



Hearsay: Strange Tales from the Middle Kingdom.

RC 48027.

by Barbara Ann Porte.

read by Celeste Lawson.

1 cassette.

Fifteen stories with Chinese themes. In "Two-Parasol

Person," Su Ling's mother insists that Su Ling always carry

two umbrellas. Years later she finds out why. In "Chinese

Ghost in America," Candice's mother tells her about a

Chinese ancestor, Ma Ku, whose mother refused to let Ma

Ku's feet be bound. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 48027.

##



Danny's Desert Rats.

RC 48264.

by Phyllis Reynolds Naylor.

read by Brian Conn.

1 cassette.

In this sequel to _Being Danny's Dog (RC 48263)_, T.R.

describes how he, his older brother Danny, and their

friends at Rosemary Acres condominium complex spend their

summer. Calling themselves the Desert Rats, the youngsters

aim to protect Paul's forbidden cat who has traveled

fifteen miles to get back to Paul. For grades 3-6. 1998.

RC 48264.

##



The Water Horse.

RC 48417.

by Dick King-Smith.

read by Larry Shapiro.

1 cassette.

Scotland, 1930. Kirstie and her little brother Angus bring

an odd fish egg home from the beach. When a strange

creature with a long neck and a horse-like head hatches in

their bathtub, their grandfather helps the children care

for the baby sea monster. For grades 3-6. 1990.

RC 48417.

##



Dancing with Great-Aunt Cornelia.

RC 48418.

by Anne Quirk.

read by Mimi Bederman.

1 cassette.

Every year around her birthday, Cornelia has tea with her

eccentric great-aunt Cornelia Witherspoon and her wolfhound

at a hotel in Manhattan. On Cornelia's thirteenth birthday,

her aunt invites Cornelia to spend the summer with her.

Cornelia meets some colorful people, and a long-hidden

family secret is revealed. For grades 4-7. 1997.

RC 48418.

##



The Family under the Bridge.

RC 48442.

by Natalie Savage Carlson.

read by Laura Giannarelli.

1 cassette (Reissue).

Armand, an adventurous old hobo, lives under a bridge in

Paris. One December day he arrives there to discover three

redheaded children and a dog. Armand cares for the young

Calcets while their mother works. During one of their

escapades around the city, they tell Santa Claus they want

a real home. For grades 3-6. 1958.

RC 48442.

##



Sun, Moon, and Stars.

RC 48448.

retold by Mary Hoffman.

read by Susan McInerney.

1 cassette.

A sampling of legends and myths created by people of

ancient civilizations to explain what they saw in the sky.

Retold tales that are still remembered in Egypt, Latvia,

Korea, Greece, and American Indian societies, among others.

For grades 4-7. 1998.

RC 48448.

##



Second Stringer.

RC 48610.

by Thomas J. Dygard.

read by Dan Bloom.

1 cassette.

All through high school Kevin Taylor has been the backup

quarterback to Rob Montgomery, mopping up in the last

quarter. When Rob is injured during the first game and out

for the season, Kevin has to step up to the challenge. Rob

encourages him to succeed. For grades 6-9 and older

readers. 1998.

RC 48610.

##



Stone Fox.

RC 48664.

by John Reynolds Gardiner.

read by Michael Kramer.

1 cassette.

Ten-year-old Willy enjoys life with his grandfather on a

small potato farm in Wyoming, until the elderly man seems

to give up on life. Willy is determined to keep the farm

going and harvests an entire crop with just his dog,

Searchlight. Now he must earn $500 to pay the taxes. For

grades 3-6. 1980.

RC 48664.

##



Heaven.

RC 48669.

by Angela Johnson.

read by Tracy Mickens-Hundley.

1 cassette.

Fourteen-year-old Marley is content with her life in

Heaven, Ohio, until a letter arrives from a church in

Alabama. Marley is shocked to find out that her parents are

really her aunt and uncle--and she feels that they have

been deceiving her. For grades 5-8. Coretta Scott King

Award. 1998.

RC 48669.

##



Adventures of Monkey King.

RC 48670.

retold by R.L. Gao.

read by Celeste Lawson.

1 cassette.

Twelve stories telling how a stone monkey becomes the

Monkey King, how he learns magic tricks from an immortal,

and how he helps Monk Tang on his journey. Retelling of

sixteenth-century Chinese folk tales. For grades 3-6. 1989.

RC 48670.

##



McBroom Tells the Truth.

RC 48671.

by Sid Fleischman.

read by Michael Kramer.

1 cassette (Reissue).

A tall tale told by Josh McBroom about the one-acre farm he

bought from Heck Jones for his wife, Melissa, and their

eleven children. When Heck sees the amazing crops Josh

grows, he schemes to take back the land. For grades 2-4.

1966.

RC 48671.

##



Riddle-Master: The Complete Trilogy.

RC 48692.

by Patricia A. McKillip.

read by Annie Wauters.

5 cassettes.

Three works originally published in the 1970s: _The Riddle-

Master of Hed_, _Heir of Sea and Fire_, and _Harpist in the

Wind_. Morgon, the Prince of Hed, accompanied by Deth the

harpist, sets out to discover the meaning of the three

stars above his brow. For grades 5-8. 1999.

RC 48692.

##



The Trolls.

RC 48714.

by Polly Horvath.

read by Susan McInerney.

1 cassette.

Aunt Sally arrives with her suitcases and stories to take

care of Melissa, age ten, Amanda, age eight, and Pee Wee,

age six, while their parents go on vacation. She tells

family stories about growing up in Canada that their father

never told them, especially the tale of the trolls. For

grades 3-6. 1999.

RC 48714.

##



Cart and Cwidder: The Dalemark Quartet, Book 1.

RC 48849.

by Diana Wynne Jones.

read by Patricia Kilgarriff.

2 cassettes.

Traveling performers Clennen and his family frequently

deliver news, messages, and passengers between the warring

states of North and South Dalemark. When Clennen is

murdered, his children wonder if a passenger is connected

in any way. A "Guide to Dalemark" is recorded with Book 4.

Prequel to _Drowned Ammet (RC 48850)_. For grades 6-9.

1975.

RC 48849.

##



If You Only Knew: The Friendship Ring, Volume 1.

RC 48853.

by Rachel Vail.

read by Kristin Allison.

1 cassette.

Seventh-grader Zoe Grandon used to be a tomboy, but her

interests are changing. For one thing, she is developing a

crush on longtime friend Tommy Levit, which just might be a

problem when Tommy asks her best friend, CJ, for a date.

Prequel to _Please, Please, Please (RC 48854)_. For grades

5-8. 1998.

RC 48853.

##



The Mouse of Amherst.

RC 48862.

by Elizabeth Spires.

read by Kimberly Schraf.

1 cassette.

When a white mouse named Emmaline moves into the poet Emily

Dickinson's bedroom, Emmaline is inspired to write verses

also. Includes eight poems by Emily Dickinson. For grades

3-6. 1999.

RC 48862.

##



No Effect.

RC 48940.

by Daniel Hayes.

read by L.J. Ganser.

2 cassettes.

Eighth graders aren't usually on the high school wrestling

team, but the coach needs more light-weight wrestlers and

soon Tyler and Lymie are in training. For Tyler the

physical pain of the sport parallels the emotional turmoil

of his crush on a teacher. Sequel to _Eye of the Beholder

(RC 42919)_. For grades 6-9. 1994.

RC 48940.

##



Jane on Her Own: A Catwings Tale.

RC 48950.

by Ursula K. Le Guin.

read by Janis Gray.

1 cassette.

When Jane, a winged cat, flies off to the city in search of

adventure, she learns that the warnings about human beings

are true. Some of them cannot be trusted. Sequel to

_Wonderful Alexander and the Catwings (RC 40491)_. For

grades 2-4. 1999.

RC 48950.

##



Marlfox.

RC 48966.

by Brian Jacques.

read by David Palmer.

2 cassettes.

Redwall Abbey is attacked by the Marlfoxes, who plunder a

wondrous tapestry for their mother, Queen Silth. Young

squirrels Dann and Song and the shrew Dippler are joined by

the watervole Burble in their perilous quest to return the

wall hanging to its proper place. Companion to _Redwall (RC

29729)_. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 48966.

##



A Wrinkle in Time.

RC 48972.

by Madeleine L'Engle.

read by Madelyn Buzzard.

1 cassette (Reissue).

Meg Murry, her younger brother Charles Wallace, and her

neighbor Calvin are transported to the planet Camazotz as

they search for Meg's lost father, a scientist studying

time travel. Prequel to _A Wind in the Door (RC 41596)_.

Newbery Medal. For grades 5-8. 1962.

RC 48972.

##



For the Life of Laetitia.

RC 48990.

by Merle Hodge.

read by Samarra Mbenga.

1 cassette.

Twelve-year-old Laetitia "Lacey" Johnson has passed the

Common Entrance Examination and will be the first one in

her family to go to high school. But in order to attend,

she must move to La Puerta to live with her father, whom

she hardly knows. For grades 6-9. 1993.

RC 48990.

##



River Thunder.

RC 49064.

by Will Hobbs.

read by Ilona Dulaski.

1 cassette.

Jessie and her friends from _Downriver (RC 36801)_ are back

to raft down the Grand Canyon. They discover that the trip

is paid for by rich kid Troy but decide to have fun anyway-

-only they are unaware of the record-high water because of

mismanagement and bad weather. For grades 6-9 and older

readers. 1997.

RC 49064.

##



Zooman Sam.

RC 49065.

by Lois Lowry.

read by Rick Rohan.

1 cassette.

In preparing for Future Job Day at Sam Krupnik's nursery

school, his parents and sister Anastasia help him put

together a zookeeper's costume.  In the process Sam learns

to sound out some words. Companion to _See You Around, Sam!

(RC 43709)_. For grades 3-6. 1999.

RC 49065.

##



Stealing Freedom.

RC 49079.

by Elisa Carbone.

read by Ilona Dulaski.

2 cassettes.

Maryland, 1850s. Young slave girl Ann Maria suffers many

hardships, including separation from her family. An

abolitionist helps her escape to Canada on the Underground

Railroad. Based on a true case. For grades 6-9. 1998.

RC 49079.

##



Sammy Keyes and the Skeleton Man.

RC 49112.

by Wendelin Van Draanen.

read by Abigail Dorn.

1 cassette.

Halloween night turns out to be especially frightening for

seventh grader Sammy and two girlfriends when a skeleton

rushes by them near a creepy house. The front door opens,

revealing a fire. Sammy puts it out and finds a tied-up

masked man. Now Sammy has a mystery to investigate. For

grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49112.

##



Can You Feel the Thunder?.

RC 49128.

by Lynn E. McElfresh.

read by Gary Tipton.

1 cassette.

Seventh-grader Mic Parsons has mixed feelings about his

fifteen-year-old deaf and blind sister Stephanie. And if he

doesn't pass mathematics, his parents won't let him try out

for baseball. After Stephanie helps Mic learn fractions, he

comes to appreciate her in a new way. For grades 5-8. 1999.

RC 49128.

##



Jack and Jill.

RC 49149.

by Louisa May Alcott.

read by Mitzi Friedlander.

2 cassettes.

Jack and Jill are the best of friends and when they are

severely injured in a sledding accident they become even

closer. With the help of Jack's mother, the two are

provided with many amusements to pass the Christmas season.

But will Jill ever walk again? For grades 5-8. 1880.

RC 49149.

##



Dogstar.

RC 49150.

by Beverley Wood and Chris Wood.

read by Jack Fox.

2 cassettes.

Jeff Beacon, thirteen, is unable to get over the death of

his bullterrier, Buddy. So his parents  take him on an

Alaskan cruise. When they go ashore in Juneau, Jeff

discovers a statue of another bullterrier, Patsy Ann,

Juneau's official boat greeter. Before long, Jeff finds

himself transported back to 1933. For grades 5-8. 1997.

RC 49150.

##



The Empress of Elsewhere.

RC 49176.

by Theresa Nelson.

read by Erik Sandvold.

2 cassettes.

The start of summer is sad for Jim Junior; his best friend

moves away and his dog dies. Then the richest lady in their

East Texas town offers him and his seven-year-old sister,

Mary Al, a challenging job: to tame her granddaughter and

her monkey. For grades 4-7. 1998.

RC 49176.

##



Hans Brinker; or, The Silver Skates.

RC 49179.

by Mary Mapes Dodge.

read by Erik Sandvold.

2 cassettes (Reissue).

In the winter Hans and his sister Gretel enjoy skating on

frozen canals in Holland, even though they are so poor they

have only clumsy, wooden skates. They would like to win the

silver skates offered as the prize in a race. Originally

published in 1865. For grades 4-7. 1993.

RC 49179.

##



Son of the Black Stallion.

RC 49195.

by Walter Farley.

read by Erik Sandvold.

2 cassettes (Reissue).

When Alec Ramsey's friend, an Arabian sheikh, sends him the

Black Stallion's foal, Alec feels that his dream has come

true. But training Satan, the high-spirited animal, is

harder than Alec anticipates. Companion to _The Black

Stallion (RC 44178)_. For grades 4-7. 1947.

RC 49195.

##



Orp.

RC 49386.

by Suzy Kline.

read by Rick Rohan.

1 cassette.

In this sequel to _Orp and the Chop Suey Burgers (RC

45368)_, Orville Rudemeyer Pygenski, Orp for short, starts

an I Hate My Name club. He's surprised when friends want to

join, but really shocked by the group that comes to the

club's first and last meeting. For grades 3-6. 1989.

RC 49386.

##



Ghost of a Hanged Man.

RC 49412.

by Vivian Vande Velde.

read by John Polk.

1 cassette.

As he is sentenced to hang for murder, outlaw Jake Barnette

vows to wreak vengeance on his accusers. Some months later

a fortune-teller warns the sheriff's children of impending

danger but also assures them that love will save them. That

prediction is borne out in a terrifying but redemptive

conclusion. For grades 4-8. 1998.

RC 49412.

##



A Traitor among Us.

RC 49413.

by Elizabeth Van Steenwyk.

read by Brian Conn.

1 cassette.

1944. In the German-occupied Netherlands, thirteen-year-old

Pieter's hatred of the Nazis gradually involves him in ever

more dangerous work for the underground resistance. Now,

with a wounded American soldier hidden in his house, he

especially fears betrayal by an unidentified village

traitor. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49413.

##



A Season of Comebacks.

RC 49415.

by Kathy Mackel.

read by Nicole Taylor.

1 cassette.

Ten-year-old Molly Burrows competes with her softball-star

older sister, Allie, for the approval of their father, who

is also Allie's softball coach. Events take a turn, Molly

makes the team, and things come together for all the family

in the final game. For grades 3-6. 1997.

RC 49415.

##



Forgive the River, Forgive the Sky.

RC 49423.

by Gloria Whelan.

read by Ann Hodapp.

1 cassette.

After her father's death, twelve-year-old Lily Star and her

mother move from their beloved home on Sandy River into an

apartment. Lily befriends the new home owner, a wheelchair-

using former test pilot. Together, Lily and her friend heal

from their tragedies. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49423.

##



Dolphin Island.

RC 49424.

by Arthur C. Clarke.

read by Randy Davidson.

1 cassette.

In this suspenseful science fiction tale, sixteen-year-old

Johnny Clinton is rescued by dolphins after the hovership

he had stowed away on crashed in the Pacific. For grades 6-

9. 1963.

RC 49424.

##



Soup in Love.

RC 49432.

by Robert Newton Peck.

read by Gary Tipton.

1 cassette.

In February Rob and his best friend Soup go to Norma Jean

Bissell's party--their first girl and boy party--to meet

some newcomers. From then on, Soup and Rob are much more

interested in Valentine's Day. Companion to _Soup (RC

33541)_. For grades 5-8. 1992. 1992.

RC 49432.

##



Jungle Dogs.

RC 49434.

by Graham Salisbury.

read by Jack Fox.

1 cassette.

In Hawaii, twelve-year-old Boy Regis is taunted as a

"sissy" by his older brother, Damon, because Boy is afraid

of wild dogs and refuses to defend himself against bullies.

When Damon takes up a fight with gang members on Boy's

behalf, events escalate and change both brothers' lives.

Some violence. For grades 5-8. 1998.

RC 49434.

##



Mountain Bike Mania.

RC 49444.

by Matt Christopher.

read by Brian Conn.

1 cassette.

Sixth-grader Will Matthews, a bored couch potato, decides

to join the school's mountain bike club, even though his

best friend Danny, an avid hiker, hates the sport. Will

becomes enthralled with his bike but soon the actions of

one irresponsible member threaten the existence of the

club. For grades 4-7. 1998.

RC 49444.

##



The Hoboken Chicken Emergency.

RC 49681.

by Daniel Pinkwater.

read by Terence Aselford.

1 cassette (Reissue).

Arthur Bobowicz goes to pick up his family's Thanksgiving

turkey at the butcher's and finds it's not there because of

a mix-up. But resourceful Arthur ends up with a choice

chicken--it just happens to weigh 266 pounds and cannot be

returned. For grades 3-6. 1977.

RC 49681.

